-- #1508 REGRESSION PIN — an INFERRED cross-module obligation must survive into the
-- importer's own inferred scheme on the `checkModules` driver.
--
-- 🚨 WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS, AND WHY THE OTHER EIGHT COULD NOT CATCH IT.  Every
-- other fixture in this corpus gives its entry bindings an explicit signature or an
-- interface-method type, so the entry's context is DECLARED and reaches the importer
-- through the module seed.  None of them exercises an obligation that is INFERRED on
-- the exporting side and must be carried across the boundary by
-- `crossModuleSchemeOblsQualRef` — the table `cmCheckWorker` was keying under `""`.
-- The corpus was blind to this shape by construction, which is exactly how 9/9 stayed
-- green through a change that alters the answer this driver gives.
--
-- MEASURED, both arms, same `MEDAKA_ROOT` and same fixture, only the probe differing:
--   before #1508:  g : a -> String            ← the `Display a =>` obligation is DROPPED
--   after  #1508:  g : Display a => a -> String
-- and the independent oracle `medaka check` (which routes through `analyzeProject`,
-- an untouched driver that always carried the real module id) agrees with the second.
--
-- `g` is deliberately UNSIGNATURED: its whole job is to make the checker infer the
-- context from `f`'s imported scheme.  Do not add a signature to it — that would
-- restore the blindness this fixture exists to remove.
import p.{f}

g v = f v

main = println (g 3)
